filter_module.s:
Make src/lib/server/qdrant.ts able to serve messaging. Four separate changes to two files. Nothing else in the repo changes.

WHY A SEPARATE FILTER MODULE: the Qdrant filter builders (`eq`, `f`) currently live in src/lib/server/qdrant.ts, which imports `@qdrant/js-client-rest`. The pure message helpers in the next step need those builders AND must be importable by a plain `node --test` file with zero dependencies. So the builders move to their own dependency-free module and qdrant.ts re-exports them, leaving every existing `import { eq, f } from '$lib/server/qdrant'` in the repo working untouched.

CHANGE 1 — create src/lib/filter.ts with exactly this content:

export type Cond =
	| { key: string; match: { value: string | number } }
	| { key: string; match: { text: string } }
	| { key: string; range: { gte?: number; lte?: number } };

export type Filter = { must: Cond[]; should?: Cond[] };

export const eq = (key: string, value: string | number): Cond => ({ key, match: { value } });
export const txt = (key: string, text: string): Cond => ({ key, match: { text } });
export const rng = (key: string, gte?: number, lte?: number): Cond => ({
	key,
	range: { ...(gte === undefined ? {} : { gte }), ...(lte === undefined ? {} : { lte }) }
});
export const f = (...conds: Cond[]): Filter => ({ must: conds });
export const f_or = (musts: Cond[], any_of: Cond[]): Filter => ({ must: musts, should: any_of });

`f_or` is the whole trick behind cross-chat search: Qdrant treats a filter that has both `must` and `should` as `must AND (at least one should)`. Verified live against Qdrant 1.18.3.

CHANGE 2 — in src/lib/server/qdrant.ts, delete these six lines:

export type Cond =
	| { key: string; match: { value: string | number } }
	| { key: string; range: { gte?: number; lte?: number } };
export const eq = (key: string, value: string | number): Cond => ({ key, match: { value } });
export const f = (...conds: Cond[]) => ({ must: conds });

and put in their place:

export { eq, txt, rng, f, f_or } from '../filter';
export type { Cond, Filter } from '../filter';

Add `import type { Filter } from '../filter';` to the top of the file, beside the existing `import { QdrantClient } from '@qdrant/js-client-rest';`. The type re-export must use `export type`, because verbatimModuleSyntax is on.

CHANGE 3 — in the same file, replace the whole `scroll` function, which currently reads:

export async function scroll(
	env: QEnv,
	filter: ReturnType<typeof f>,
	limit = 1000
): Promise<Pt[]> {
	const r = await (await qc(env)).scroll(C, {
		filter,
		limit,
		with_payload: true,
		with_vector: false
	});
	return r.points as Pt[];
}

with:

export async function scroll(
	env: QEnv,
	filter: Filter,
	limit = 1000,
	order_by?: { key: string; direction: 'asc' | 'desc' }
): Promise<Pt[]> {
	const r = await (await qc(env)).scroll(C, {
		filter,
		limit,
		with_payload: true,
		with_vector: false,
		...(order_by ? { order_by } : {})
	});
	return r.points as Pt[];
}

Ordering by a payload key is how the inbox lists newest-first and how a thread reads oldest-first. It requires the ordered key to carry an index, which change 4 provides.

CHANGE 4 — in the same file, replace the whole `ensure` function, which currently reads:

let ensured = false;
export async function ensure(env: QEnv): Promise<void> {
	if (ensured) return;
	const c = await qc(env);
	await c.createPayloadIndex(C, { field_name: 'g', field_schema: 'keyword' }).catch(() => {});
	await c.createPayloadIndex(C, { field_name: 's', field_schema: 'keyword' }).catch(() => {});
	await c.createPayloadIndex(C, { field_name: 't', field_schema: 'keyword' }).catch(() => {});
	ensured = true;
}

with:

let ensured = false;
export async function ensure(env: QEnv): Promise<void> {
	if (ensured) return;
	const c = await qc(env);
	for (const k of ['g', 's', 't', 'cv', 'mf', 'mt', 'ma', 'mb', 'pg'] as const)
		await c.createPayloadIndex(C, { field_name: k, field_schema: 'keyword' }).catch(() => {});
	await c.createPayloadIndex(C, { field_name: 'md', field_schema: 'integer' }).catch(() => {});
	await c
		.createPayloadIndex(C, {
			field_name: 'mx',
			field_schema: {
				type: 'text',
				tokenizer: 'word',
				lowercase: true,
				min_token_len: 2,
				max_token_len: 30
			}
		})
		.catch(() => {});
	ensured = true;
}

The `as const` matters: without it the loop variable widens to `string` and `field_schema: 'keyword'` no longer typechecks against the client's literal union.

CHANGE 5 — still in src/lib/server/qdrant.ts, add two new writers directly BELOW the existing `upsert` function, which ends with:

	await (await qc(env)).upsert(C, { points: points.map((p) => ({ ...p, vector: { [V]: ZV } })) });
}

Append:

export async function upsert_novec(
	env: QEnv,
	points: { id: string; payload: Record<string, unknown> }[]
): Promise<void> {
	if (!points.length) return;
	await (await qc(env)).upsert(C, { points: points.map((p) => ({ ...p, vector: {} })) });
}

export async function set_payload(
	env: QEnv,
	id: string,
	payload: Record<string, unknown>
): Promise<void> {
	await (await qc(env)).setPayload(C, { payload, points: [id] });
}

`upsert_novec` is the one that must be used for every message and conversation row — see the CRITICAL QDRANT TRAP in the preamble. `set_payload` patches a single key without re-reading and re-writing a whole row, which is what clearing an unread counter needs.

Do not touch seed.json, scripts/seed.mjs, or any route in this step.

chat_module.s:
Create the pure, dependency-free heart of messaging: conversation ids, previews, side selection, and the three Qdrant filters, including the cross-chat search filter. Nothing here touches the network, which is exactly why all of it is testable.

Create src/lib/chat.ts with exactly this content:

import { eq, txt, rng, f, f_or, type Cond, type Filter } from './filter.ts';

export type Msg = { cv: string; mf: string; mt: string; mx: string; md: number; pg: string };
export type Conv = {
	cv: string;
	ma: string;
	mb: string;
	pg: string;
	pn: string;
	mx: string;
	md: number;
	ua: number;
	ub: number;
};

export const norm = (e: string): string => e.toLowerCase().trim();

export const conv_id = (a: string, b: string): string => [norm(a), norm(b)].sort().join('|');

export const preview = (text: string): string => {
	const s = text.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();
	return s.length > 160 ? s.slice(0, 159) + '…' : s;
};

export const is_a = (c: Conv, me: string): boolean => c.ma === norm(me);
export const peer_of = (c: Conv, me: string): string => (is_a(c, me) ? c.mb : c.ma);
export const unread_key = (c: Conv, me: string): 'ua' | 'ub' => (is_a(c, me) ? 'ua' : 'ub');
export const unread_of = (c: Conv, me: string): number => Number(c[unread_key(c, me)] ?? 0);
export const in_conv = (c: Conv, me: string): boolean => c.ma === norm(me) || c.mb === norm(me);

export const conv_filter = (me: string): Filter =>
	f_or([eq('s', 'adca'), eq('t', 'cv')], [eq('ma', norm(me)), eq('mb', norm(me))]);

export const search_filter = (me: string, q: string): Filter =>
	f_or(
		[eq('s', 'adca'), eq('t', 'm'), txt('mx', q.trim())],
		[eq('mf', norm(me)), eq('mt', norm(me))]
	);

export const thread_filter = (cv: string, after?: number): Filter => {
	const conds: Cond[] = [eq('s', 'adca'), eq('t', 'm'), eq('cv', cv)];
	if (after) conds.push(rng('md', after + 1));
	return f(...conds);
};

Three things about this file that are load-bearing and must not be "tidied":
- the import path is './filter.ts' WITH the extension. Its test file is run by plain `node --test`, which cannot resolve an extensionless relative import.
- `search_filter` deliberately carries NO `cv` condition. That absence is what makes it search across every conversation at once rather than inside one. Its `should` pair is what keeps a person's search inside their own messages.
- `thread_filter` uses `gte: after + 1` rather than a `gt`, because the shared `rng` helper only models `gte`/`lte` and `md` is an integer count of milliseconds. A falsy `after` (including 0) adds no range condition at all, so a first load fetches the whole thread.

Then place the planner's test file with exactly this command, and do not open, retype or edit it:

cp plan/tests/src/lib/chat.test.ts.txt src/lib/chat.test.ts

`pnpm test` must go from 45 passing to 62 passing.

chat_server.s:
Create the server side of messaging: the only module in the repo that reads or writes message and conversation rows.

Create src/lib/server/chat.ts with exactly this content:

import { scroll, retrieve_one, upsert_novec, set_payload, uuid_from, ensure, type QEnv } from './qdrant';
import {
	conv_id,
	preview,
	norm,
	unread_key,
	conv_filter,
	search_filter,
	thread_filter,
	type Conv,
	type Msg
} from '$lib/chat';

export const conv_uuid = (cv: string): Promise<string> => uuid_from('conv:' + cv);

export async function get_conv(env: QEnv, id: string): Promise<Conv | null> {
	const pt = await retrieve_one(env, id);
	if (!pt?.payload || pt.payload.t !== 'cv') return null;
	return pt.payload as unknown as Conv;
}

export async function list_convs(env: QEnv, me: string): Promise<Conv[]> {
	await ensure(env);
	const pts = await scroll(env, conv_filter(me), 200, { key: 'md', direction: 'desc' });
	return pts.map((p) => p.payload as unknown as Conv);
}

export async function get_thread(env: QEnv, cv: string, after?: number): Promise<Msg[]> {
	await ensure(env);
	const pts = await scroll(env, thread_filter(cv, after), 500, { key: 'md', direction: 'asc' });
	return pts.map((p) => p.payload as unknown as Msg);
}

export async function search_msgs(env: QEnv, me: string, q: string): Promise<Msg[]> {
	if (!q.trim()) return [];
	await ensure(env);
	const pts = await scroll(env, search_filter(me, q), 60, { key: 'md', direction: 'desc' });
	return pts.map((p) => p.payload as unknown as Msg);
}

export async function send_msg(
	env: QEnv,
	from: string,
	to: string,
	text: string,
	pg: string,
	pn: string
): Promise<{ id: string; cv: string; m: Msg }> {
	await ensure(env);
	const mf = norm(from);
	const mt = norm(to);
	const cv = conv_id(mf, mt);
	const md = Date.now();
	const m: Msg = { cv, mf, mt, mx: text, md, pg };
	await upsert_novec(env, [{ id: crypto.randomUUID(), payload: { s: 'adca', t: 'm', ...m } }]);
	const id = await conv_uuid(cv);
	const was = await get_conv(env, id);
	const [ma, mb] = cv.split('|');
	const next: Conv = {
		cv,
		ma,
		mb,
		pg: was?.pg || pg,
		pn: was?.pn || pn,
		mx: preview(text),
		md,
		ua: Number(was?.ua ?? 0),
		ub: Number(was?.ub ?? 0)
	};
	next[unread_key(next, mt)] += 1;
	await upsert_novec(env, [{ id, payload: { s: 'adca', t: 'cv', ...next } }]);
	return { id, cv, m };
}

export async function mark_read(env: QEnv, c: Conv, me: string): Promise<void> {
	const k = unread_key(c, me);
	if (!Number(c[k] ?? 0)) return;
	await set_payload(env, await conv_uuid(c.cv), { [k]: 0 });
}

// ponytail: unread_total scrolls every conversation the user is in on every page load.
// Fine at this scale; move the counter into its own row, or a Durable Object like x2's
// ChatHub, if a person ever has hundreds of threads.
export async function unread_total(env: QEnv, me: string): Promise<number> {
	const convs = await list_convs(env, me);
	return convs.reduce((n, c) => n + Number(c[unread_key(c, me)] ?? 0), 0);
}

Points that must not be changed:
- both writes go through `upsert_novec`, never `upsert`. See the CRITICAL QDRANT TRAP in the preamble.
- `send_msg` writes the message row FIRST and the conversation row second, so a crash between the two loses a preview, never a message.
- `send_msg` keeps the original `pg`/`pn` of an existing conversation (`was?.pg || pg`) so a thread that began on one product does not silently rebrand itself when the same two people talk again from a different page.
- `mark_read` uses `set_payload` and returns early when there is nothing to clear, so opening a thread costs one write at most and usually zero.
- the `ponytail:` comment above `unread_total` is the only comment permitted in this file. Keep it; it names a real ceiling.

There is no test file for this step: every function here needs a live Qdrant. Its pure parts are already covered by src/lib/chat.test.ts.

msg_api.s:
Create the one HTTP endpoint messaging speaks through: POST sends a message, GET polls a thread for anything newer than the caller already has.

Create src/routes/api/msg/+server.ts with exactly this content:

import type { RequestHandler } from './$types';
import { error, json } from '@sveltejs/kit';
import { env } from '$env/dynamic/private';
import { retrieve_one, uuid_from } from '$lib/server/qdrant';
import { get_conv, get_thread, send_msg } from '$lib/server/chat';
import { in_conv, norm, peer_of } from '$lib/chat';

export const POST: RequestHandler = async ({ request, locals }) => {
	if (!locals.user) throw error(401, 'sign in to send a message');
	const body = (await request.json()) as { pg?: string; id?: string; x?: string };
	const text = (body.x ?? '').trim().slice(0, 4000);
	if (!text) throw error(400, 'write something first');
	const me = norm(locals.user.e);

	if (body.id) {
		const c = await get_conv(env, body.id);
		if (!c || !in_conv(c, me)) throw error(403, 'not your conversation');
		const r = await send_msg(env, me, peer_of(c, me), text, c.pg, c.pn);
		return json({ id: r.id, m: r.m });
	}

	const slug = (body.pg ?? '').trim();
	if (!slug) throw error(400, 'no product given');
	const pt = await retrieve_one(env, await uuid_from(slug));
	const p = pt?.payload as Record<string, string> | undefined;
	if (!p || p.t !== 'p') throw error(404, 'no such product');
	const owner = norm(p.e ?? '');
	if (!owner) throw error(400, 'this product has no builder to message yet');
	if (owner === me) throw error(400, 'that is your own product');
	const r = await send_msg(env, me, owner, text, slug, p.n ?? slug);
	return json({ id: r.id, m: r.m });
};

export const GET: RequestHandler = async ({ url, locals }) => {
	if (!locals.user) throw error(401, 'sign in');
	const id = (url.searchParams.get('id') ?? '').trim();
	if (!id) throw error(400, 'no conversation given');
	const c = await get_conv(env, id);
	const me = norm(locals.user.e);
	if (!c || !in_conv(c, me)) throw error(403, 'not your conversation');
	return json({ m: await get_thread(env, c.cv, Number(url.searchParams.get('after') ?? 0) || 0) });
};

The security shape here is the part that matters and must not be loosened:
- every branch requires `locals.user`. There is no anonymous messaging; the unauthenticated path for a cold investor is the existing interest form, which stays.
- the caller NEVER names the person they are writing to. They name a product slug, and the server looks up that product's private `e` key. That key is never sent to the browser, so a caller cannot address an arbitrary email, and cannot learn one.
- to continue an existing thread the caller passes the conversation row's id, and `in_conv` proves they are one of its two participants before anything is written or read. Guessing another person's conversation id is guessing a SHA-256-derived uuid.
- messaging yourself is refused, so a builder cannot open a thread against their own product.
- text is trimmed and hard-capped at 4000 characters at this trust boundary.

inbox_list.s:
Build /inbox: every conversation this person is in, and the search box that reads the words of every message they have ever sent or received, across all of those conversations at once. This is the step the whole "search across chats" requirement lands in.

FILE 1 — create src/routes/inbox/+page.server.ts with exactly this content:

import type { PageServerLoad } from './$types';
import { redirect } from '@sveltejs/kit';
import { env } from '$env/dynamic/private';
import { list_convs, search_msgs, conv_uuid } from '$lib/server/chat';
import { norm, peer_of, unread_of } from '$lib/chat';

export const load: PageServerLoad = async ({ locals, url }) => {
	if (!locals.user) throw redirect(302, '/google?next=/inbox');
	const me = norm(locals.user.e);
	const q = (url.searchParams.get('q') ?? '').trim();
	const convs = await list_convs(env, me);
	const rows = await Promise.all(
		convs.map(async (c) => ({
			id: await conv_uuid(c.cv),
			pg: c.pg,
			pn: c.pn,
			who: peer_of(c, me),
			mx: c.mx,
			md: c.md,
			un: unread_of(c, me)
		}))
	);
	if (!q) return { q, rows, hits: [] };
	const by_cv = new Map(convs.map((c) => [c.cv, c]));
	const hits = await Promise.all(
		(await search_msgs(env, me, q)).map(async (m) => {
			const c = by_cv.get(m.cv);
			return {
				id: c ? await conv_uuid(c.cv) : '',
				pn: c?.pn ?? '',
				who: m.mf === me ? m.mt : m.mf,
				mine: m.mf === me,
				mx: m.mx,
				md: m.md
			};
		})
	);
	return { q, rows, hits: hits.filter((h) => h.id) };
};

Two deliberate details: hits whose conversation row is missing are dropped, so a stray message can never link nowhere; and the conversation list is always loaded, even during a search, so the page keeps its shape and a search can be cleared without a second thought.

